# Secrets of the backend. Copy to /etc/zk-bridge/backend/backend.env, mode 0640, owner root, group zkb_backend.
# The values come from the 1Password vault of the deployment. backend.json names these variables
# (runpodApiKeyVariable, webhookBearerVariable); it never holds the values.
#
# Its partner is /etc/zk-bridge/backend/backend.json (backend.json.example here), same mode and owner, because its
# endpoints can carry keys. Notes for that file on zkb.fi:
#   publicUrl            https://webhook.zkb.fi (the prover posts to <publicUrl>/webhook/jobs/<id>; Caddyfile ZKB_WEBHOOK_HOST)
#   trustForwardedHeader true, with trustedProxyHops 1: Caddy is the one proxy in front of the backend
#   indexerUrl           http://127.0.0.1:42069, the indexer on this server. The requests module asks it whether a
#                        proof is needed before it starts a paid job, and the archive module reads its work list
#   prover.binary        /opt/zk-bridge/current/bin/zk-bridge-prover, where deploy.sh installs it

# The key of the remote proving service (RunPod).
RUNPOD_API_KEY=
# The bearer value that the prover sends to the webhook. At least 32 bytes, for example: openssl rand -hex 32.
# The RunPod worker holds the same value.
BACKEND_WEBHOOK_BEARER=
